melband-roformer (0.1.14) unstable; urgency=medium

  * app/melband_roformer_wrapper/progress.py (new): rewrite the terminal
    progress output of the wrapped `melband-roformer-infer` subprocess.
    Previously two uncoordinated, uninformative signals reached the
    terminal as-is: a `tqdm(desc="Tracks", unit="track")` bar that only
    advances once per *completed file* (with one input file it prints
    0% at start and jumps straight to 100% at the end, never animating),
    and a separate plain-text "Estimated time remaining: ..." line with
    no bar. Both are now replaced by a single bar, sized by total input
    audio duration (seconds, via soundfile) rather than track count, so
    it fills smoothly in real time from the same per-chunk ETA figures
    upstream already computes, and correctly reflects proportional
    progress across multi-file batches. The finished line is shortened
    to `Tracks: 100%|<bar>| 1/1 [24.20s/track]` (drops the
    `N/N [elapsed<remaining, rate]` clutter). Implemented by running the
    subprocess behind a pty and filtering its \r/\n-delimited output
    (upstream emits no ANSI escapes, so no full terminal emulation is
    needed); every other line (device/CUDA banner, "Processing track
    i/N: ...", warnings, tracebacks, "Elapsed time: ...") passes through
    unchanged. Affects both the deb and AppImage terminal UI and the GUI
    (gui.py shells out to this same `melband-roformer` CLI).
  * app/requirements.txt, app/pyproject.toml: add tqdm>=4.64 as a direct
    dependency (progress.py imports it directly; previously only present
    transitively via melband-roformer-infer's own dependency on it).

 -- melband-roformer packaging <packaging@example.invalid>  Sun, 27 Sep 2026 12:00:00 +0000

melband-roformer (0.1.13) unstable; urgency=medium

  * appimage/melband-roformer-hud-term.py: fix Ctrl+Shift+C/Ctrl+Shift+V
    (copy/paste) not working, and every keypress silently clearing the
    current text selection instead, when a non-Latin (e.g. Cyrillic
    ЙЦУКЕН) keyboard layout is active. Root cause: _on_key() matched the
    chord against event.keysym.lower() == "c"/"v" -- but X11 keysym is
    what the *active layout* maps a key to, not the physical key itself.
    Under a Cyrillic layout the physical C/V keys report keysyms
    "Cyrillic_es"/"Cyrillic_em" (they type С/М), which never equal
    "c"/"v", so the copy/paste branch was skipped and execution fell
    through into the "any other keystroke dismisses the selection"
    branch right below it -- reproducing exactly the reported behaviour
    (selection cleared on every keypress; only the right-click context
    menu still worked, since it calls _copy_selection()/
    _paste_clipboard() directly and never goes through this keysym
    check).
    Fix: added _is_copy_chord()/_is_paste_chord(), which match primarily
    on event.keycode (54/55 -- the X11 hardware keycodes for the
    physical C/V key positions under the evdev driver, layout-
    independent) and fall back to keysym (now covering both the Latin
    and the two common Cyrillic keysym names) for the rare setups where
    keycodes don't follow that mapping. _on_key() now calls these
    instead of the old literal keysym.lower() == "c"/"v" checks.
    Verified with a standalone reproduction of the matching logic
    against synthetic (keysym, keycode) pairs for both Latin and
    Cyrillic layouts, and against a keycode-mismatch case, since this
    environment has no X display to drive the real Tk widget through.
  * appimage/melband-roformer-hud-term.py: fix a second, more
    fundamental cause of the same symptom, present even on a Latin
    layout: pressing Ctrl or Shift *on its own* (as its own physical
    keypress, before the letter of the chord is pressed) already cleared
    the selection. X11 delivers Ctrl+Shift+C as three separate KeyPress
    events (Control_L, then Shift_L, then C/Cyrillic_es), and
    event.state on each event reflects the modifier state *before* that
    key -- so the Control_L keypress itself still has ctrl=False in its
    own event.state. That event matched none of the chord checks and
    fell through to the same "any other keystroke dismisses the
    selection" branch, clearing the selection on the very first keydown
    of the sequence, before Ctrl+Shift+C could ever be recognized as a
    chord. Fix: _on_key() now recognizes bare-modifier keysyms
    (Control_L/R, Shift_L/R, Alt_L/R, Meta_L/R, Super_L/R, Hyper_L/R,
    ISO_Level3_Shift, ISO_Level5_Shift, Mode_switch, Caps_Lock,
    Shift_Lock, Num_Lock, Scroll_Lock) as a no-op -- neither clearing
    the selection nor sending anything to the child -- so the selection
    survives until the actual chord letter arrives. Verified with a
    standalone simulation of the full Ctrl-then-Shift-then-C keypress
    sequence (both Latin and Cyrillic) confirming the selection now
    survives the two modifier keydowns and the copy still fires on the
    third, plus a regression check that an ordinary keystroke (no
    modifiers) still clears the selection as intended.

