melband-roformer (0.1.11) unstable; urgency=medium

  * app/sitecustomize.py: extend the venv-wide deprecated-API patch (added
    in 0.1.7 for torch.backends.cuda.sdp_kernel) to also cover
    torch.cuda.amp.autocast(), used by upstream
    mel_band_roformer/utils.py. Observed on a real GPU run:
      utils.py:86: FutureWarning: `torch.cuda.amp.autocast(args...)` is
      deprecated. Please use `torch.amp.autocast('cuda', args...)` instead.
        with torch.cuda.amp.autocast():
    Same fix shape as sdp_kernel: replace torch.cuda.amp.autocast itself,
    at interpreter start-up, with a same-signature shim
    (_CudaAmpAutocastShim, supporting enabled=/dtype=/cache_enabled=,
    usable both as a context manager and as a decorator) built on the new,
    non-deprecated torch.amp.autocast('cuda', ...), defaulting dtype to
    float16 when not given to reproduce the old API's own default rather
    than whatever torch.amp.autocast might otherwise pick for "cuda".
    Upstream's own call site keeps working completely unmodified.
  * tests/test_cli.py: add
    test_sitecustomize_patches_deprecated_cuda_amp_autocast, mirroring
    test_sitecustomize_patches_deprecated_sdp_kernel (added in 0.1.7) --
    same subprocess_env()-based approach, so it exercises the exact
    mechanism cli.py/selftest.py rely on for the real upstream subprocess,
    not this test process's own possibly-different ambient
    `import sitecustomize` resolution. Always-run tier, CPU-only.

 -- melband-roformer packaging <packaging@example.invalid>  Fri, 25 Sep 2026 11:00:00 +0000

melband-roformer (0.1.10) unstable; urgency=medium

  * scripts/build-deb.sh: skip `apt-get update` entirely when every
    build-dep in BUILD_DEPS is already installed (new
    _all_build_deps_installed(), checked via `dpkg-query -W -f='${Status}'`
    per package), instead of always running it whenever `--no-deps` isn't
    explicitly passed. `apt-get update` refreshes *every* apt source
    configured on the machine, not just the two Debian ones this package
    actually needs (deb.debian.org, security.debian.org) -- on a real
    desktop that routinely also means unrelated third-party repos (GPU
    vendor, browser, remote-desktop, chat client, etc.), several of which
    can be slow or flaky to reach and none of which ship any of
    BUILD_DEPS. A real build log showed this step being the longest-
    hanging part of the whole invocation while reporting "Обновлено 0
    пакетов, установлено 0 новых пакетов" -- i.e. a real network round
    trip for zero effect, since the packages were already present.
    `--no-deps` already let you skip this manually; this makes the script
    make that same call automatically and correctly instead of relying on
    the caller to remember the flag. Applied to both the root and
    plain-user (`sudo apt-get ...`) code paths identically via the shared
    BUILD_DEPS array and helper, so both stay in sync going forward.

 -- melband-roformer packaging <packaging@example.invalid>  Fri, 25 Sep 2026 10:00:00 +0000

melband-roformer (0.1.9) unstable; urgency=medium

  * debian/control: drop the unused ${shlibs:Depends} substvar from the
    Depends field. dh_shlibdeps is (correctly, see override_dh_shlibdeps's
    own comment) run with --exclude=usr/lib/melband-roformer/venv, since
    the venv's vendored .so files (torch's bundled cuBLAS/cuDNN/NCCL,
    numpy/scipy extensions) must not be resolved against Debian's shared
    library database. The only files this package installs *outside*
    that excluded venv are debian/melband-roformer{,-gui}.launcher, both
    plain POSIX shell scripts, plus .desktop/.svg/man/doc files -- no
    ELF at all. dh_shlibdeps therefore never has anything to analyze and
    never writes a shlibs:Depends= line into
    debian/melband-roformer.substvars (unlike misc:Depends/misc:Pre-Depends,
    which override_dh_makeshlibs echoes explicitly). Referencing
    ${shlibs:Depends} in control when nothing in the build can ever
    populate it was always going to warn at dpkg-gencontrol time:
      "substitution variable ${shlibs:Depends} used, but is not defined"
    Confirmed by an actual 0.1.8 build. Since this isn't a build
    misconfiguration but the correct, intended shape of the package
    (bundled runtime, no system-library deps to express), the fix is to
    stop declaring a substvar the build will never define, not to fake
    one. No functional change to the shipped binary or its real runtime
    dependencies (ffmpeg + ${misc:Depends} already cover them).

 -- melband-roformer packaging <packaging@example.invalid>  Fri, 25 Sep 2026 09:00:00 +0000

melband-roformer (0.1.8) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Fix two bugs found via an actual failed build on real hardware (0.1.7
    never having been build-tested for real before this):
  * app/melband_roformer_wrapper/__init__.py: subprocess_env() was
    computing its PYTHONPATH override by doing `import sitecustomize` in
    the *current* process and reading that module's __file__ -- but
    ambient `import sitecustomize` resolution is exactly the mechanism
    subprocess_env() exists to route around (see its docstring), so on a
    host where ambient resolution finds someone else's sitecustomize.py,
    this computed the WRONG directory and silently defeated the whole
    fix. Rewritten to derive the path purely from this package's own
    on-disk location (melband_roformer_wrapper/ and sitecustomize.py are
    installed by the same wheel into the same site-packages dir, see
    app/pyproject.toml's py-modules) -- no import, no ambient resolution,
    deterministic regardless of sys.path.
  * tests/test_cli.py::test_sitecustomize_patches_deprecated_sdp_kernel
    had the identical bug at the test level: it asserted on the *test
    process's own* ambient `import sitecustomize` + direct
    torch.backends.cuda.sdp_kernel() call, rather than on what production
    code (cli.py/selftest.py) actually relies on. This passed in sandbox
    testing but FAILED on a real machine where the test process's own
    ambient resolution found a different sitecustomize.py (very plausibly
    from one of the many third-party apt repos e.g. NVIDIA CUDA/
    VirtualBox/etc. such a desktop install tends to have configured).
    Rewritten to spawn a subprocess via subprocess_env(), exactly the way
    cli.py/selftest.py launch the real melband-roformer-infer subprocess,
    so the test now exercises (and only trusts) the actual production
    mechanism.
  * scripts/build-deb.sh: the $SUDO_USER/project-owner ownership-fixup
    added in 0.1.7 only covered $PROJECT_ROOT and ~/.cache/pip; a real
    build additionally hit `/tmp/pytest-of-$USER` (pytest's own tmp root,
    created by debian/rules' override_dh_auto_test) being root-owned from
    an earlier root-run build, which pytest's tmp_path fixture refuses to
    reuse ("OSError: The temporary directory ... is not owned by the
    current user"), aborting the whole test tier (and hence the build).
    Refactored the ownership-fixup logic into a shared
    _fix_stale_root_ownership_everywhere() helper and broadened its
    coverage from ~/.cache/pip to all of ~/.cache (covers any other cache
    tooling ever writes there, not just pip) plus
    ${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/pytest-of-$USER, applied in both the $SUDO_USER path
    and the direct-root/project-owner path added in 0.1.7.

 -- melband-roformer packaging <packaging@example.invalid>  Thu, 24 Sep 2026 20:00:00 +0000

melband-roformer (0.1.7) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Fix `FutureWarning: torch.backends.cuda.sdp_kernel() is deprecated`
    printed on every separation run. The warning comes from upstream
    melband-roformer-infer's attention code, not from our own wrapper --
    and the actual separation runs as a *subprocess* (cli.py execs the
    `melband-roformer-infer` console-script directly), so a monkeypatch
    inside melband_roformer_wrapper itself would never reach it. Added
    app/sitecustomize.py, installed as a top-level module (new
    `[tool.setuptools] py-modules` in app/pyproject.toml) so it lands in
    the venv's own site-packages, where Python's `site` machinery
    auto-imports it at the start of *every* interpreter invocation in
    this venv -- our CLI, our GUI, and upstream's own console-scripts
    alike. It replaces torch.backends.cuda.sdp_kernel() with a
    same-signature shim built on the new, non-deprecated
    torch.nn.attention.sdpa_kernel(), so upstream's existing call site
    keeps working unmodified but no longer touches the deprecated code
    path. Added tests/test_cli.py::test_sitecustomize_patches_deprecated_sdp_kernel
    (always-run tier, CPU-only) asserting the context manager no longer
    raises under `warnings.simplefilter("error", FutureWarning)`.
  * scripts/build-deb.sh: when the root->$SUDO_USER drop-privileges step
    (added in 0.1.5) runs, also fix ownership of any pre-existing
    root-owned files under $SUDO_USER's own ~/.cache/pip before handing
    off. An earlier root-run build (from before that drop existed, or
    from a plain `sudo dpkg-buildpackage`/`sudo pip ...` bypassing this
    script) can leave root-owned entries there; once the build correctly
    drops to $SUDO_USER, pip can read but not write alongside them,
    which surfaces as "WARNING: The directory '.../.cache/pip' ... is
    not owned or is not writable by the current user" and silently
    disables the cache rather than failing the build outright. Same fix
    applied to $PROJECT_ROOT itself, since a previous root-run build can
    just as easily leave out/, .venv-build/, or dpkg-buildpackage's own
    debian/melband-roformer/, debian/.debhelper/ etc. root-owned -- that
    would otherwise fail a later $SUDO_USER-based build with a hard
    permission error only *after* redoing the whole (expensive) build.
  * scripts/build-deb.sh: also handle a genuine root login/`su -` (no
    `sudo`, so no $SUDO_USER to drop to -- previously this case just
    warned and built as root unconditionally). Now falls back to
    dropping to whichever user already owns the checkout (overwhelmingly
    likely to be the intended non-root build user), same ownership
    fixups as the $SUDO_USER path above, guarded by `sudo` actually being
    installed and the owner being a real, non-root account; otherwise
    still falls through to building as root with the existing warning.

